**Read:** [Gas flow standards and their uncertainty — Wright and colleagues](../sources/Gas-Flow-Standards-NIST.lingua#r_2FJV0VJ3K710PNV7E45JGGJXG5), static and dynamic gravimetric methods and uncertainty discussions.

Mass change over time can provide a gas-flow realization, but the weighing system measures forces on its complete assembly. Buoyancy, connecting-line forces, thermal effects, evaporation or condensation outside the intended gas boundary can alter the indication.

Distinguish static before/after weighing from weighing while flow continues. Dynamic operation adds flow forces and balance-response questions; disconnecting between measurements can alter the enclosed material and surface state. Define precisely which mass difference belongs to the gas transferred.

Molar flow inferred from mass requires the mixture molar mass. Composition uncertainty matters even if the mass difference is precise. In a collecting system, connecting-volume storage and leakage still need accounting; choosing mass rather than volume does not eliminate the control-volume problem.

**Work:** Build a hypothetical mass-change budget with a gas transfer, buoyancy change and hose force. Show which effects cancel in a carefully matched difference and which do not. Compare the resulting uncertainty with a PVTt realization over the same flow range and duration.

**Return:** [Industrial gases, vacuum and high-purity supplies — from molecules to useful service](../Industrial-gases-and-vacuum.lingua#r_3GHQJEYMYEN6JDKSF4PPJ403RF).
